+# FAA and NORAD questions for the record and a June 2004 time clarification
+
+**November 9, 2026 | B129 / PD01 / PD07.** Three Commission archive scans add the actual June 2003 NORAD questions, a May 2003 briefing-material shipping lead, and a June 2004 FAA email acknowledging and correcting a time interpretation. The 2004 correction concerns the Tampa–Lexington flight inquiry. The corrected answer about the FAA headquarters phone bridge and its delivery remain separate targets.
+
+## June 2003 NORAD question set
+
+**Marcus Box 8 item 491** preserves two physical pages. The web-print counters read **Page 1 of 3** and **Page 2 of 3**. The question face names NORAD as respondent, gives **June 18, 2003** as the production deadline and ties the request to the **May 23, 2003** hearing. The first question asks when FAA–NORAD open lines were established, their duration and any recordings or transcripts. The second question visibly says **“United Airlines flight 77”** and gives **9:24 am** as the reported notification time. The FAA May 22 exchange and May 23 written answer instead concern **American Flight 77**. Preserve the question's aircraft wording and its stated premise alongside the FAA record. The body ends with **June 4, 2003** and the General Counsel's printed name. Physical p. 1, lines 5–21 and printed counter, and p. 2, lines 13–17 and printed counter. [Exact member and source checks](SOURCE-PINS.json) · [Prior May hearing and June 2 email review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/830a98d1158dba74c47371a5fa6be56453e18b87/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-hearing-drafts-and-follow-up-2026-11-06/README.md)
+
+The first page has a handwritten June marking of unverified origin. The two body pages give seven numbered questions and contain no separate item labeled Page 3 of 3. The full web print, exact outgoing attachment, NORAD receipt and its response remain open. The held June 2 email reports that the questions had yet to reach the liaison and were being finalized. The June 4 dated document supplies the text of a later question set. Its date alone leaves send and receipt unverified.
+
+## Pre-hearing briefing CD lead
+
+**GSA Box 128 item 903**, **RDOD 04017655**, carries Bates **NCT0072356–NCT0072357**. P. 2 continues a **May 9, 2003** message seeking help with analysis for the Continental United States NORAD commander's May 23 hearing. The **May 12** exchange says the analysis was too large to email, had been put on a **CD**, and was intended for next-day courier shipment. It provides a shipping route to check. Printed May 12 time fields **2:33 PM**, **2:29 PM** and **3:25 PM** remain tied to their own nested messages. The scan leaves their timezone offsets, actual shipment, media contents and use in testimony open. Physical p. 1 lines 4, 13, 42, 53–54 and 61–63, and p. 2 lines 10–29 and Bates footer. [Exact member locator](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## June 2004 FAA response and correction trail
+
+The **Team 5 Box 69** compilation has a signed June 8 cover at physical p. 12 identifying the **May 25, 2004 FAA Questions for the Record No. 1**. The cover uses **“maintains”** in its passage about FAA air traffic data, as does the **Official File Copy** cover in AWA-335 p. 20. AWA-335 p. 1 uses **“tracks”**. The Team 5 signed cover and the official file copy therefore share this wording while retaining different visible form and signature fields. Preserve those observations alongside the separate signed p. 1 cover. Physical p. 12 lines 11 and 22–24 and checked cover image. [AWA-335 cover comparison](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7601900) · [Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+- **June 16, 2004.** The staff question asks whether **20:38** on September 13, 2001 is Zulu and asks separately about special authorizations. Earlier in the thread, staff reports an approximately **16:37** Tampa Airport time and asks whether it could refer to another departure. The FAA reply references the spreadsheet **9-11commission_flts_follow_up.xls**. The scan preserves printed tables and the attachment reference. Its PDF embedded-file count is zero. Physical pp. 4–7, p. 4 lines 14–27 and 52, and p. 5 lines 24–27.
+- **Printed rows.** Physical pp. 9–10 give **HPJ32 / 41251 / TPA → LEX / 20:38** for September 13. P. 10 separately gives **HPJ32 / 41976 / LEX → TPA / 22:43**. The question's **16:37**, the printed **20:38**, and the later **16:38** are separate source fields. The printed database responses give a specific flight-index pair to check against the native workbook and airport logs. P. 9 lines 1–16, and p. 10 lines 5–12.
+- **June 18, 2004, 10:29 AM.** The printed FAA email to Commission staff says the initial time information was incorrect, says the times are Zulu and gives **20:38 → 16:38 or 4:38pm** for the Tampa–Lexington flight. It reports leaving a voicemail. It attributes the restriction records sought in questions 2–5 to a TSA transfer and says FAA has no record of a special authorization for the flight and return. The 240-minute conversion is the arithmetic shown by the correction's two labels. It does not calibrate the source clock or prove identity with the airport entry. The original airport record, voicemail, native workbook, exact dispatch and recipient acknowledgment remain open. Physical p. 1 lines 4, 13–21 and 53–54. [Exact source and locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+These are distinct chains for a NORAD hearing question set, a military briefing input, and the FAA's June 2004 airspace-closure follow-up. The June 18 response supplies an example of a concrete written correction with date, subject, addressee and corrected field. The May 2003 answer at issue in the DOT OIG referral concerns when the Air Force liaison joined the FAA headquarters bridge. The June 2004 chain does not supply that correction or the administrative decision. [DOT OIG report, printed pp. 10–12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=10)
+
+## Verification and remaining originals
+
+Forty new physical pages were screened via extracted text and page margins. Visual reading was targeted to both NORAD question pages, the two briefing correspondence pages, and the FAA correction, follow-up, selected tables and response cover. Hash and CRC checks apply to the acquired archive members. The 2009 acquisition's concurrent process wrote two verified member bodies before the aggregate receipt failed on a third filename from the 2011 index. The held bodies were independently rechecked against the 2009 index. A separate completed 2011 acquisition has its own receipt. Original PDFs, contact fields and signature images are outside the public derivative. [Source pins and limits](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+1. Seek the NORAD question attachment with its full print/export, reply and actual receipt, and the hearing-analysis CD and shipment records. Check which data, draft and personnel actually informed the response.
+2. Seek the June 2004 original email, workbook, dispatch and recipient acknowledgment, plus the underlying airport and federal records to compare timezone and flight-index fields.
+3. Keep the May 2003 FAA written answer, corrected version and receipt, signed administrative decision, and recommendation-level outcomes as priority originals.
